WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.080
Just because kids will figure out a way&nbsp;
to see "The Suicide Squad" doesn't mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.640
they'll understand everything they see in the&nbsp;
movie. Here are some "R-rated" or otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.880
"adult" moments in "The Suicide Squad" that&nbsp;
will soar over the heads of younger viewers.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.760
Although they're never seen, we are informed&nbsp;
that Nazis built Jotunheim after World War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.880
II, when they came to Corto Maltese seeking asylum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.040
and hoping to continue their experiments.&nbsp;
Couple this with the eventual reveal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.360
Jotunheim's inner workings, where faceless&nbsp;
prisoners are held in obscene conditions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.160
shuffling their feet in cramped cells, and you&nbsp;
get a fictional horror reflecting a real one.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.360
For an adult, such imagery&nbsp;
coupled with the words "Nazi"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.960
brings to mind the most repulsive&nbsp;
horrors mankind has ever witnessed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.080
including painful, often deadly experiments on&nbsp;
thousands of prisoners without their permission.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.680
Of course, none of this involved an&nbsp;
enormous starfish from outer space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.600
But as an adult, there's a reason why&nbsp;
words like "Nazi" and "experiment"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.760
carry connotations with such power that&nbsp;
a blockbuster filmmaker would invoke them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.040
when trying to elicit a sense&nbsp;
of repulsion from his audience.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.720
So, while the superhero subject&nbsp;
matter may sometimes be silly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.160
that doesn't make it any less of a good&nbsp;
opportunity to engage the young person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.800
in your life about the very real horrors&nbsp;
that once unfolded here on planet Earth.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.120
When an adult hears the very first words&nbsp;
spoken in this movie — "Hello, I'm Johnny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:22.080
Cash" — perhaps they'll realize they're hearing&nbsp;
"Folsom Prison Blues," a song the legendary Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:28.320
in Black performed to a crowd of inmates at Folsom&nbsp;
State Prison in 1968. Against the wishes of many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.720
this performance — and the groundbreaking live&nbsp;
album that came with it — gave inmates hope and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:38.000
made them feel like human beings, something very&nbsp;
important to Cash, who was a hero to the working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.120
class, downtrodden, and incarcerated&nbsp;
until the day he died. Appropriately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:48.480
the song plays over footage of Savant sitting&nbsp;
in prison, occupying himself with a bouncy ball.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:52.880
In the same vein, when an adult hears the rousing&nbsp;
track "People Who Died" play over the opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.600
credits, perhaps they'll realize that this is&nbsp;
a song by poet and punk rocker Jim Carroll,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.520
and that all those people in the song&nbsp;
really did die in the ways being described.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.800
For greater detail about many of&nbsp;
those unfortunate acquaintances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.480
of Carroll — born to a working-class&nbsp;
Irish family in New York's Lower East&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:13.360
Side — perhaps the adult may have read Carroll's&nbsp;
autobiographical book "The Basketball Diaries,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:21.840
or at least seen the 1995 Leonardo DiCaprio film&nbsp;
of the same name that starred Leo as Carroll.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.520
"I need some money real bad."

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:30.560
From Louis Prima to Pixies, the "Suicide Squad"&nbsp;
soundtrack is as catchy as it is a gateway to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:35.120
multiple generations, and the multiple&nbsp;
subtexts these musicians bring to mind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:39.280
James Gunn is a master at using pop&nbsp;
music to give added dimension to a scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.480
and he selects most of his&nbsp;
soundtracks while he writes his films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.720
creating scenes around the feelings and&nbsp;
associations that a song carries with it.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:51.360
To a younger person, maybe the songs just sound&nbsp;
cool. So this is your chance to talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:54.400
music old and new — and the more&nbsp;
they know about these musicians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.960
the more they'll appreciate the&nbsp;
film next time they watch it.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.160
A couple decades back, an urban legend spread&nbsp;
from coast to coast about a certain movie star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.800
(who will not be named here — you have search&nbsp;
engines) who supposedly had a thing for gerbils.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:11.280
It's not true, but it evidently captured&nbsp;
the imagination of an entire generation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.160
many of whom probably still&nbsp;
think there's something to it.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.840
Flash-forward to today and you have an&nbsp;
antagonistic dynamic in "The Suicide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:21.920
Squad" between Ratcatcher II and the&nbsp;
Thinker. In one contentious moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.360
she asks him, "Do you want a dozen&nbsp;
angry rodents crawling up your ass?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.640
to which he responds, "My answer&nbsp;
may not be what you expect."

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.400
The exchange seems more likely to get&nbsp;
a laugh from an adult than a teen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.560
possibly because that adult remembers the&nbsp;
pre-internet days when the urban legend was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.200
spread from locker room to locker room. It&nbsp;
also perhaps reflects a generational divide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.720
with an older person more likely to&nbsp;
laugh at another's perceived "kink,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.920
while younger generations are&nbsp;
probably more apt to expect anything.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.200
So, teens, tolerate your mom and dad if they laugh&nbsp;
at that joke — there's a whole history behind it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:57.200
Adults, don't be upset if your teen says you&nbsp;
shouldn't laugh at such things — tolerance and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.920
acceptance are never bad instincts to possess,&nbsp;
even when we're simply overanalyzing a throwaway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.720
line about the sex life of a fictional evil genius&nbsp;
with wires and metal sticking out of his head.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:12.960
To a younger person, Viola Davis' Amanda Waller&nbsp;
is the quasi-bad guy who deploys Task Force X on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:17.120
their mission, and blows up their heads if they&nbsp;
try to escape. But if you're an adult who knows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:21.360
anything about the source material, you're aware&nbsp;
that Waller has been the driving force of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:27.120
modern Suicide Squad since the late '80s, and is&nbsp;
far and away its most morally complex character.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:32.320
In 1987's "Secret Origins" #14, we&nbsp;
learned that Waller grew up in Chicago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:37.440
had five kids — and then watched her son killed,&nbsp;
daughter attacked, and husband slaughtered while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:42.160
trying to avenge them. When the police refused to&nbsp;
help, she had the wherewithal and determination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.480
to go back to school, use her education&nbsp;
to land a job with the federal government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:51.360
and then embark on a mission to make sure&nbsp;
"metahumans" would never hurt civilians again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.720
Kinda gives you pause when you consider&nbsp;
that Waller being knocked unconscious by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.400
her mutineering staff is played as&nbsp;
a triumphant moment worth cheering.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:02.240
Want to engage the young person in your life&nbsp;
in a conversation about the shortcomings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.240
of law enforcement? The desire to take the&nbsp;
law into your own hands — and why that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:11.120
make the problem worse? The slippery slope of&nbsp;
vengeance? The moral implications of treating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:16.000
prisoners as something less than human beings&nbsp;
with rights? Amanda Waller is your gateway.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.480
While it's easy to watch a superhero film and say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.720
"Batman is a vigilante. Isn't that cool?"&nbsp;
it's a lot more important (and hopefully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:27.920
rewarding) to delve into the moral complexities&nbsp;
of such characters. Sooner or later, every kid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:32.640
needs to learn that most people aren't good or&nbsp;
bad, but instead dwell in the grey area between.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:34.960
"I'm a superhero!"

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:40.640
Perhaps no superhero series depicts characters&nbsp;
in that grey area better than the "Suicide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:45.120
Squad" movies, where villains are heroes,&nbsp;
heroic figures hide their darkest secrets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:48.640
and the monster at the end of the&nbsp;
movie is a sullen interstellar captive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:52.720
subjected to decades of rendition. Who&nbsp;
knows? Such a discussion might not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:56.560
be good parenting, but it might also give&nbsp;
them a deeper appreciation for the movie.

